---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const breathCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '2600 + (tv/2)*(1 - cos(2*pi*f*x/60))', label: 'lung volume', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'f', label: 'breathing rate', min: 8, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'breaths/min' },
    { name: 'tv', label: 'tidal volume', min: 400, max: 2500, step: 50, value: 500, unit: 'mL' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 12, label: 'time (s)' },
  y: { min: 1000, max: 4200, label: 'lung volume (mL)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const labCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'f', label: 'breathing rate', min: 8, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'breaths/min' },
    { name: 'tv', label: 'tidal volume', min: 400, max: 2500, step: 50, value: 500, unit: 'mL' },
    { name: 'sa', label: 'alveolar surface area', min: 40, max: 100, step: 5, value: 70, unit: 'm²' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'minute ventilation', expr: 'f*tv/1000', unit: 'L/min' },
    { label: 'alveolar ventilation (150 mL dead space)', expr: '(tv - 150)*f/1000', unit: 'L/min' },
    { label: 'relative O₂ diffusion capacity (∝ area)', expr: 'sa/70', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const ventCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'diaphragm flattens, external intercostals lift ribs', note: 'Inhaling: the diaphragm contracts and flattens; the external intercostal muscles pull the ribs up and out.' },
    { math: 'thorax volume ↑ → pressure ↓', note: 'Boyle\'s law in a chest: more volume means lower pressure inside the lungs — now below atmospheric.' },
    { math: 'air flows IN down the pressure gradient', note: 'Air is pushed in by the atmosphere itself; the lungs do not suck.' },
    { math: 'muscles relax (quiet) — internal intercostals contract (forced)', note: 'Exhaling at rest is elastic recoil: the diaphragm relaxes and domes up. During exercise, the internal intercostals pull ribs down to force air out faster.' },
    { math: 'volume ↓ → pressure ↑ → air flows OUT', note: 'Smaller chest, higher pressure than the atmosphere, air leaves. Ventilation is just bulk flow down pressure differences — no biology required until the alveoli.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/gas-exchange-in-humans">
  <h2>The idea: a surface built for exchange</h2>
  <p>
    Every cell in your body burns fuel with oxygen and excretes carbon dioxide, which makes gas
    exchange a logistics problem: the air outside holds the supply, the blood is the delivery
    system, and somewhere the cargo must transfer between them. The transfer point is the
    <strong>alveoli</strong> — roughly 300 million microscopic air sacs clustering at the ends of
    the bronchial tree like grapes. They follow the classic four-point recipe for any exchange
    surface: a <strong>large surface area</strong> (about <strong>70 m²</strong> in an adult,
    half a tennis court folded into your ribcage), a <strong>thin barrier</strong> (each alveolus
    and its capillary share a wall one cell thick, so diffusion distances are under a
    micrometre), a <strong>good blood supply</strong> to keep the concentration gradients steep
    on the blood side, and <strong>good ventilation</strong> to keep refreshing the air side.
    Oxygen diffuses in, carbon dioxide diffuses out, and nothing has to chase anything — the
    gradients do the work, powered by nothing but the second law of thermodynamics.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rest of the respiratory system is scaffolding for that surface. The airways —
    <strong>trachea, bronchi, bronchioles</strong> — form a branching tree from one tube to 300
    million terminals; only at the alveolar endings does exchange happen, and everything above
    them is plumbing. Rings of <strong>cartilage</strong> hold the trachea open like a hosepipe
    (without them every breath would collapse the airway to zero); <strong>goblet cells</strong>
    secrete the mucus that traps pathogens and particles before they reach the delicate alveoli;
    <strong>ciliated cells</strong> sweep that mucus-laden debris upward to the throat to be
    swallowed. The plumbing defends the factory.
  </p>

  <h2>Ventilation: how air is moved</h2>
  <p>
    Mammals can't rely on diffusion to move air over any distance — air must be pumped. The
    mechanism is pure physics once you see it:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="inhale, exhale — five steps from muscle contraction to bulk flow" widget="equationStep" config={ventCfg} />

  <p>
    The result is a rising and falling lung volume. At rest you breathe about
    <strong>15 times a minute</strong> with a <strong>tidal volume near 500 mL</strong>; in
    hard exercise both climb (rates above 30 per minute, tidal volumes of 2–3 litres). Note the
    asymmetry: inhalation is always <em>active</em> (muscles must contract to enlarge the chest),
    but exhalation at rest is <em>passive</em> — the diaphragm simply relaxes and the elastic
    lung tissue recoils like a released balloon. Only during exercise do the internal
    intercostals and abdominal muscles actively force air out. Drag the sliders to trace what
    your lungs are doing right now versus mid-sprint:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="lung volume against time — resting breathing vs exercise; the wiggle height is tidal volume, its frequency is breathing rate" widget="plotter" config={breathCfg} />

  <p>
    The two dials — rate and depth — are not interchangeable, because of <strong>dead
    space</strong>: about 150 mL of air sits in the trachea and bronchi where no exchange
    happens, and it must be moved every breath before any fresh air reaches the alveoli. Shallow
    panting is therefore inefficient: 30 breaths of 300 mL moves 9 litres a minute, but
    (300 − 150) × 30 = only 4.5 L of it ventilates the alveoli. Deep breathing of 8 breaths at
    1,500 mL also moves 9 litres, but (1,500 − 150) × 8 = 10.8 L reaches the alveoli —
    <em>more</em> effective with less total airflow. This is why athletes are trained to breathe
    deep and slow, and why the respiratory centre in exercise raises <em>both</em> dials.
  </p>

  <h2>Inspired vs expired air</h2>
  <p>
    The air you breathe out is not the air you breathed in. The classic test is
    <strong>limewater</strong>, which turns milky in carbon dioxide: breathe through a straw
    into a flask of limewater and it clouds within seconds, while the control flask left open to
    room air barely changes. A cold mirror held to the lips fogs with expired breath because
    expired air is saturated with water vapour. The standard composition figures:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> about <strong>21%</strong> inspired → about <strong>16%</strong> expired — only about a quarter of the oxygen is extracted per breath.</li>
    <li><strong>Carbon dioxide:</strong> about <strong>0.04%</strong> inspired → about <strong>4%</strong> expired — a hundredfold rise.</li>
    <li><strong>Water vapour:</strong> variable in inspired air → expired air is <strong>saturated</strong> (alveoli are wet).</li>
    <li><strong>Nitrogen:</strong> about 78% in both — it takes no part in respiration.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The small oxygen extraction is worth pausing on. Air breathing is a flow system with a
    buffer: because each breath only removes a quarter of the oxygen, the alveolar gas stays
    around 14–16% oxygen even at rest — a reservoir that keeps the diffusion gradient strong
    even between breaths. The hundredfold CO₂ rise is the exhaust signature: metabolism produces
    CO₂ in exact proportion to O₂ consumed, and the lung exports it all. These numbers are also
    diagnostic tools: expired-air resuscitation (rescue breathing) works precisely because
    expired air still contains 16% oxygen — enough to keep a casualty's blood oxygenated. And
    the limewater test scales up: anaesthetists monitor exhaled CO₂ continuously during surgery,
    because a sudden drop signals a circulatory or airway crisis before any other instrument
    notices. Two flasks and a straw at school; the same chemistry, read in real time, in every
    operating theatre.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="how much air actually reaches the alveoli — plus how surface area scales diffusion" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />

  <h2>Transport and diffusion: pressures and gradients</h2>
  <p>
    Diffusion only runs downhill, and at altitude the hill gets smaller. "Air at 21% oxygen"
    means a partial pressure of about 21 kPa at sea level; at 5,500 m it is under 10 kPa, which
    is why mountaineers hyperventilate and why altitude training increases red-cell mass. In the
    alveolus, oxygen at roughly 13–14 kPa diffuses into blood arriving at about 5 kPa (venous,
    after the tissues have taken their share); in the tissues the gradients reverse. The blood's
    carrying agent is <strong>haemoglobin</strong>: each red cell packs about 270 million
    haemoglobin molecules, each binding four O₂ molecules, which multiplies the oxygen capacity
    of blood by roughly 70-fold over what could dissolve in plasma alone. Haemoglobin is
    exquisitely tuned: nearly saturated at lung pressures, but releasing oxygen readily in
    active tissues where it is slightly warmer, more acidic and CO₂-rich (the Bohr effect) —
    exactly where the oxygen is needed. CO₂ travels mostly as bicarbonate ions in the plasma
    (about 70%), some bound to haemoglobin (about 23%), a little dissolved (7%) — chemistry you
    will meet again in the excretion lesson when blood pH comes up.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "you breathe to get oxygen." You breathe to get rid of
    carbon dioxide. The breathing reflex is governed primarily by blood CO₂ (via its effect on
    blood acidity), not by oxygen lack — which is why holding your breath becomes irresistible
    long before your oxygen runs low, and why hyperventilating before diving (which flushes CO₂
    and suppresses the urge to breathe) is genuinely dangerous: you can black out from oxygen
    starvation while still feeling no need to breathe.</p>

  <h2>Defence of the airway</h2>
  <p>
    Every cubic metre of urban air carries millions of particles — pollen, dust, bacteria, soot —
    and almost none of it reaches the alveoli. The first filter is the nose: hairs and turbinates
    that warm, humidify and strain the air. In the trachea and bronchi, the mucus escalator takes
    over: goblet cells secrete a sticky carpet that traps anything larger than a few micrometres,
    and cilia beat it upward at about 1–2 cm per minute — a slow, relentless conveyor that moves
    the entire mucus lining, swallowed reflexively many times an hour. Particles small enough to
    reach the alveoli meet patrolling macrophages that engulf them and ride the escalator out.
    Smoke defeats all of these at once: it paralyses the cilia for hours, inflames the airways,
    and loads the mucus with tar, which is why smokers' lungs accumulate the debris the escalator
    would normally clear, and why they suffer chronic bronchitis (inflamed, mucus-flooded
    airways) and emphysema — which literally dissolves alveolar walls, merging hundreds of sacs
    into a few large ones and destroying the surface area the whole system exists to provide.
    Surface area lost cannot be rebuilt; the 70 m² is a one-time endowment.
  </p>

  <h2>Exercise, CO₂ and the brain's CO₂ meter</h2>
  <p>
    Working muscles respire faster, and the waste — carbon dioxide — is what drives you to
    breathe harder. The chain is worth memorising in order:
    <strong>respiration rises → blood CO₂ concentration rises → the brain detects the increase
    → it signals the breathing muscles → rate and depth both increase</strong> → the extra
    ventilation flushes the CO₂ out. Notice it is a negative-feedback loop with the brain as
    the sensor and the diaphragm and intercostals as the effectors — you never decide to
    breathe harder during a sprint; the CO₂ decides for you. The sensors sit in the medulla of
    the brainstem and in the aortic and carotid bodies, and they respond to CO₂ dissolved in
    blood as it lowers pH — the brain is, in effect, running a pH meter. Exercise ventilation
    can rise twenty-fold, from about 6 L/min to 120 L/min, while oxygen uptake rises about
    fifteen-fold; at maximum effort ventilation tracks the CO₂ load almost perfectly, which is
    why elite athletes stop when CO₂ (and acid), not oxygen, becomes the limiting factor.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The quantitative core of the system is Fick's law: <strong>diffusion rate ∝ surface area ×
    concentration difference ÷ thickness</strong>. Every feature of the alveolus is a term in
    that equation, and every respiratory disease attacks one. Emphysema removes surface area;
    pneumonia floods alveoli with fluid, thickening the barrier; pulmonary fibrosis scars and
    thickens it; a pulmonary embolism cuts the blood supply, collapsing the gradient. Clinical
    oxygen therapy works by raising the concentration-difference term — giving pure O₂ (partial
    pressure near 100 kPa rather than 21) steepens every gradient five-fold and can carry a
    patient over a crisis their own lungs can't manage. Premature babies lack surfactant, the
    soap-like molecule that keeps alveoli from collapsing under surface tension (without it,
    every breath costs the work of re-inflating sticky sacs) — neonatal surfactant therapy,
    introduced in the 1990s, is Fick's-law engineering at its most literal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system's oddities teach its history. Why do we breathe unidirectionally in and out
    through the same pipe, when birds run air one-way through their lungs and extract oxygen
    twice? Because mammalian lungs evolved as an outpouching of the gut — a blind sac design —
    while birds' flow-through design came from a different lineage. The mammalian plan is
    adequate at sea level but imposes the dead-space penalty and the residual-air mixing that
    caps our altitude performance. And the residual air itself (about 1,200 mL that can never be
    exhaled) is what keeps the alveoli inflated between breaths and lets gas exchange run
    continuously rather than in pulses. Even the measurement is elegant: spirometry, the
    volume-versus-time trace of a maximal breath, is the single cheapest window into lung health,
    and the FEV1 number it yields (forced expiratory volume in one second) has sorted
    asthmatics from healthy lungs for over a century. The system is a physics engine wrapped in
    biology — measure it with physics, treat it with chemistry, keep it alive with feedback.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 11; OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology, ch. 22 (The Respiratory System); West, Respiratory Physiology: The Essentials, 11th ed.</p>
</TopicPage>
